WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.840
The movie opens by showing us the decrepit
hand of Mary Shaw lighting a candle, and this

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.380
could be seen as a foreshadowing of what would
eventually happen to the theater in the movie’s

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:11.070
climax, but it may have also been chosen as
sort of a director’s trademark for James

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:11.780
Wan.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:15.300
After their breakout success with Saw, Wan
and screenwriter Leigh Whannell teamed up

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:20.130
again to make Dead Silence, which was released
in 2007, and contained many recognizable elements

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:21.970
that established Wan as an auteur.

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:25.300
The opening shot of Saw contained a small
blue light floating in the water, so Dead

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:29.980
Silence’s first shot has its colors altered
to that the flame’s light also appears blue.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.540
One of the first things we see is her blueprints
for “the perfect doll”, which are also

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:37.820
pretty similar in style to designs made by
the antagonist of Wan’s first movie.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:41.620
Dead Silence would not only bring back more
trademarks established in his previous film,

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:45.739
but would also set up conventions that wouldn’t
be expanded upon until years later in his

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:46.240
career.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.860
For this and many more Things You Missed,
make sure you stick around to the end of this

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:50.860
video.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:56.940
Am I becoming like the doll movie guy?

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:00.210
It’s not really on purpose, it just kind
of keeps happening.

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:03.670
Anyway, the opening title sequence shows Mary
Shaw flipping through a photo album.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:06.920
It’s difficult to see the details, but if
you do pay attention to them, it sets up a

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:08.999
secret that would later be unveiled.

00:01:08.999 --> 00:01:12.549
When the mystery is all but solved, we find
out that Mary Shaw’s spirit is going after

00:01:12.549 --> 00:01:16.219
the families that killed her and dismembered
her tongue in 1941.

00:01:16.219 --> 00:01:20.090
So essentially the curse is tied to your lineage,
and that's why we see her documenting each

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:24.450
family tree, from the birth of a new son in
the Ashen family, to the introduction of Aunts

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:29.479
and Uncles, the tracking of new wives and
daughters, cousins with creepy faces and more.

00:01:29.479 --> 00:01:33.130
Further on in this booklet, we see her design
sketches and blueprints jotted down about

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.659
“perfect design” and making the “perfect
doll”.

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:40.219
We see the doll parts like legs and eye triggers,
which we don’t realize are part of the construction

00:01:40.219 --> 00:01:43.469
of a human doll, unless you really look closely
at the proportions.

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:47.299
We see more of this book when Jamie discovers
it later on while exploring the theater, where

00:01:47.299 --> 00:01:50.919
there are more diagrams that seem to be plans
for the human version of the doll as well

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:53.280
as pages talking about surgery and human anatomy.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.340
I’d also like to comment on the music.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:58.940
The soundtrack was done by Charlie Clouser,
who also worked with Wan on the Saw movies,

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:03.239
and the main theme sounds like a children’s
wind up music box, which fits nicely with

00:02:03.239 --> 00:02:06.709
the whole theme of dolls, and kids being turned
into ventriloquist dummies and stuff.

00:02:06.709 --> 00:02:10.579
The main character is Jamie Ashen, and he
seems to have a passion for bicycles, judging

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:11.870
by the decorations in his apartment.

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:16.180
He has a vintage bicycle poster in the kitchen,
this “cycles Gladiator” poster, which

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:20.180
I found out is actually a type of wine, not
a bicycle brand, but still features a bicycle

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.360
on the label, and of course, actual bicycles.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:26.269
I think that the reasoning behind these choices
has less to do with actual bicycles, and more

00:02:26.269 --> 00:02:29.629
to do with the idea of Mary Shaw’s curse
being generational.

00:02:29.629 --> 00:02:33.459
Each subsequent generation of the Ashen family
represents a new life cycle, a new victim

00:02:33.459 --> 00:02:35.319
for Mary Shaw to circle around to.

00:02:35.319 --> 00:02:38.269
That’s why the first victim of the movie
appears to be his wife, but actually turns

00:02:38.269 --> 00:02:40.700
out to be the boy she was carrying.

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.969
When the package containing the ventriloquist
doll arrives at their door, Jamie’s wife

00:02:43.969 --> 00:02:46.320
Lisa makes an interesting promise to the doll.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:54.740
“If Jamie to scream, I’ll make sure you
get seven minutes in heaven with my old Barbie.

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:55.800
Deal?”

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:59.760
It’s an interesting choice of words, because
the legend of Mary Shaw warns that the victims

00:02:59.769 --> 00:03:04.349
should not scream, in fact, the movie ends
when Jamie actually does scream, and he pays

00:03:04.349 --> 00:03:05.280
the price for it.

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:08.840
"NOOOOOOOO!!!!!!!!"

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:13.060
Another potential attempt at foreshadowing
can be seen on Jamie’s fridge.

00:03:13.069 --> 00:03:17.180
And I say potentially because I can’t confidently
translate the meaning behind all of these

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:21.330
fridge magnet letters, but one thing that
does stand out to me is the word: LAKE.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:25.180
The theater where Mary Shaw’s curse began,
which is also the location of the climax of

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.709
the movie, is located on the island in Lost
Lake.

00:03:27.709 --> 00:03:31.260
But perhaps the biggest easter egg I found
in Jamie’s apartment was this, which at

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:35.499
first appeared to be a painting, but upon
closer inspection, I realized was actually

00:03:35.499 --> 00:03:36.950
a Jigsaw puzzle!

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:40.540
This is a clear connection to Saw, where the
villain would cut Jigsaw pieces out of his

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:41.220
victim’s bodies.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:45.019
When Jamie comes home from picking up takeout,
he finds his dead wife’s body hidden under

00:03:45.019 --> 00:03:48.410
a sheet, which is great for me, because I
can show some of this scene, and again, great

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:52.280
for me, because I can point out another director’s
trademark: covering a victim in a sheet to

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:57.120
create suspense was also a tactic that Wan used
in Saw, where Sing and Tapp discover Jeff

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:57.700
Ridenhour.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:01.900
There's also another cloth pull scene later in Dead Silence where it looks like the same cloth is even used.

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:07.920
This could also explain why James Wan became
friends with Lights Out director, David Sandberg.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:19.220
In the following scene, we meet the man who
would be investigating Lisa’s murder.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:22.520
We can tell he’s a cop, not by the fact
that he's in a police station, but because

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:27.240
he’s eating a donut, and we all know cops
cannot resist donuts.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:28.980
"Just refill this with jelly will ya?

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:33.020
Thanks. You're an angel."

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:37.870
When Jamie gets back into his hometown Ravens
Fair, he drives by a sign describing it as…

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:41.620
A Quiet Place, which coincidentally would
be the title of an excellent horror movie

00:04:41.639 --> 00:04:45.419
11 years later where the characters were also
at risk of being killed if they screamed…

00:04:45.419 --> 00:04:48.500
but what I actually wanted to point out was
this logo at the top of the sign.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:49.180
It's very similar

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:53.199
to a logo later seen on the grave markers
of Mary Shaw and her many puppets.

00:04:53.199 --> 00:04:57.420
This could suggest that she has a greater
influence on the town than previously disclosed.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:01.160
The town is now run-down and falling apart,
like the in-real-life equivalent of Screen

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:02.160
Rant’s reputation.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:05.030
We see mannequins in this shop window, and
we would later find more mannequins at the

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:06.030
theater.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:09.199
At this point in his career, it’s safe to
call this another director’s trademark.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:13.449
You may remember from Saw, that Jigsaw’s
workshop is hidden in a mannequin factory.

00:05:13.449 --> 00:05:16.180
"It's an old listing. Used to be a mannequin factory."

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:16.820
What can I say?

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:17.820
The dude loves mannequins!

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:20.240
But I think there’s actually something deeper
going on here.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:24.789
Let’s take a closer look at some of the
recurring elements in his films: mannequins,

00:05:24.789 --> 00:05:27.990
dolls and we later see Mary Shaw take over
a ventriloquist clown.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:31.370
There’s one thing all of these elements
have in common, they are all prompters for

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:35.979
a phenomenon known as the uncanny valley effect,
which is when something has a human likeness,

00:05:35.979 --> 00:05:40.030
but is not human, or at least has something
non-human about its appearance, causing our

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:44.010
brains to think that something is wrong, which
is why a lot of people find clowns, dolls,

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:47.270
or even the original trailer for Sonic to
be kind of creepy.

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:50.780
So I would say that’s something Wan finds
fascinating, because we’ve seen examples

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:52.789
of it come up time and time again in his work.

00:05:52.789 --> 00:05:54.990
I mean the dude’s Instagram handle is @creepypuppet.

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:59.020
Jamie’s first stop was his childhood home
to visit his father, but the surprise he would

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:01.900
find there holds major clues about the movie’s
twist.

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:15.340
When Jamie returns home, the door is answered
by Ella…

00:06:15.349 --> 00:06:16.349
Oh…

00:06:16.349 --> 00:06:17.349
Oh no.

00:06:17.349 --> 00:06:19.580
Please don’t tell me that’s your mom dude.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:22.820
“Well, I guess I’m your stepmother.”

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:27.199
*breathing through teeth* Yeah… she’s
pretty young.

00:06:27.199 --> 00:06:31.010
I guess, she’s not technically young because
of the whole part where we find out she’s

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:32.010
actually Mary Shaw.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:35.169
There may be a clue in there, in that she
has this vibrant eye color, which is shared

00:06:35.169 --> 00:06:38.090
by the creepy version of Mary Shaw and by
all of her dolls.

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:39.870
The next part I legitimately don’t get.

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:40.870
Like I don’t understand it.

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:41.860
Let me roll the clip.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:45.800
“Ella, who’s down there with you?”

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:49.520
Isn’t the whole twist that the father has
been dead the whole time and she’s controlling

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:51.879
him like a life size ventriloquist doll?

00:06:51.879 --> 00:06:53.210
So who’s saying that?

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:56.879
The only thing I can think is that she can
actually give the dolls like a voice of their

00:06:56.879 --> 00:07:00.160
own, because we do see a similar effect in
the theater flashback, where the audience

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:03.130
reacts as if Billy’s voice actually is coming
from the crowd.

00:07:03.130 --> 00:07:06.440
“You won’t find me mother.”

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:11.520
I guess the idea behind Mary Shaw’s “perfect
doll” idea is that she taxidermied the father

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:13.539
and converted him into a ventriloquist doll.

00:07:13.539 --> 00:07:17.780
I think the idea is teased at when Jamie goes
to the funeral parlor and sees Marion Walker

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:21.010
playing with a stuffed crow, another example
of the use of taxidermy.

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:24.650
But can we talk about how hilarious this lamp
at Jamie’s motel is though.

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:26.790
It’s just like a fish spitting out the lamp.

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:28.409
That’s my kind of humor.

00:07:28.409 --> 00:07:31.080
Also at the motel: scary faces behind a window
curtain?

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:32.320
Director’s trademark.

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:39.360
"Josh! Josh, Josh, please!

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:41.800
Someone's in Kali's room. Someone's in Kali's room!"

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:45.800
The next day is Lisa’s funeral, and there
are a couple of tributes hidden in the cemetery

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:46.300
set.

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:49.260
The first is this tombstone with the name:
John Musikka.

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:53.219
John Musikka is the name of an Ontario based
locations manager, but he doesn’t appear

00:07:53.219 --> 00:07:56.289
to have been involved with Dead Silence as
far as I can tell.

00:07:56.289 --> 00:08:00.440
So I wonder if this is actually a graveyard
set that has been used in another production,

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:02.310
and this headstone was just kind of left over.

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:06.570
The alternative is that he just wasn't’
credited, and if you’ve seen my last video,

00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:07.979
you know that that’s never good.

00:08:07.979 --> 00:08:11.490
Another grave marker has the name Gregory,
which was likely a tribute to the late producer

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:15.700
Greg Hoffman, who is always credited posthumously
by his friends Oren Koules and Mark Burg.

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:18.760
Jamie learns the name of the the ventriloquist doll: Billy, which

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:22.000
is also the name of the puppet used to deliver
messages to the victims in Saw.

00:08:22.010 --> 00:08:25.060
The Saw version of Billy would also make a
little cameo, later in the movie.

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:26.700
We’ll get there a little bit later on though.

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:29.979
The name of the theater that Mary used to
perform at is called the Guignol Theater.

00:08:29.979 --> 00:08:31.870
“That’s a French *** name!’

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:32.870
That is correct, Darrell.

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:36.460
The word “guignol” translates to “puppet”,
and it may more specifically be named after

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:40.350
a French theater, The Grand Guignol, which
was known for the horror productions it put

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:42.270
on in the early 1900s.

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:45.710
Seems appropriate, given all the horrific
moments in this movie, like an old woman asking

00:08:45.710 --> 00:08:49.790
to be made into a doll after her death, and
this part where her ghost can only be seen

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:51.270
passing by in the mirror.

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:54.960
But after exploring the theater and its history
and trying to re-bury Billy himself, Jamie

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:58.210
is unable to find the answers he’s looking
for about the connection between his Great

00:08:58.210 --> 00:09:01.690
Uncle Michael Ashen and the curse of the ventriloquist,
Mary Shaw.

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:04.050
He returns home again to confront his father.

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:08.140
Most of Mr. Ashen’s lines are delivered
in close-up, so that we as the audience don’t

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:10.200
see Ella’s lips moving as he’s speaking.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:14.190
However, in this shot we do get a glimpse
of it, but it’s brief enough that most probably

00:09:14.190 --> 00:09:14.880
wouldn’t notice.

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:18.500
We also see it happen in a wide shot, while
our eyes are drawn to the motion of Jamie

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:19.440
leaving the room.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:23.480
Similarly, when Jamie receives the phone call
from Henry Walker, the funeral home director

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:27.460
who was killed by Mary Shaw not long before,
Ella is conveniently not in the shot until

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:30.500
Henry’s voice stops, so again, we can’t
see her lips moving.

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:34.800
Although Detective Lipton does try to stop him,
Jamie storms off to go meet Henry at the theater.

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:36.740
“Jamie!”

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:38.560
“Jamie!”

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:40.500
"JAMIE!"

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:43.280
“Now you’re gone and I’m waiting patiently.

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:45.380
Jamie!”

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:47.500
“I don’t have a full tank of gas!”

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:50.700
Actual footage of me being nominated as a
designated driver.

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:54.240
Once they do arrive at the theater though, the
Detective finally starts to believe that the

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:58.780
ghost stories might be true when he hears
the voice of Henry coming from seemingly nowhere.

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:02.100
“Yes, he is!”

00:10:06.460 --> 00:10:09.500
“If you say I told you so, I’ll shoot
you.”

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:11.620
You know, officer, that sounds a little bit
like

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:12.700
“BRUTALITY!”

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:16.210
Jamie disappears into a secret area behind
the wall, leading them to discover that all

00:10:16.210 --> 00:10:19.270
the dolls from the cemetery have somehow been
relocated here.

00:10:19.270 --> 00:10:22.830
One of them looks a lot like an early version
of the Annabelle doll that would debut in

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:24.980
James Wan’s 2013 film, The Conjuring.

00:10:24.980 --> 00:10:28.870
Obviously, I don’t think he predicted the
future and knew he would making The Conjuring,

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:32.670
because the original plan for that movie was
to use the Raggedy Ann doll that looks like

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:36.090
this, but once it was decided that they’d
be making scarier looking doll for the film,

00:10:36.090 --> 00:10:39.400
I wouldn’t be surprised if he went back
and said, “OK, why don’t we use something

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:40.400
like this?”

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:43.240
The first doll that Mary possesses in this
scene,

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:45.090
as I mentioned earlier is the clown puppet, and it has a spiral

00:10:45.090 --> 00:10:49.390
on its forehead which is yet another connection
to Saw, where there were several spiral patterns,

00:10:49.390 --> 00:10:51.430
most notably on the cheeks of Billy the Puppet.

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:53.360
This is also the scene where Billy makes his
cameo.

00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:57.040
He can be seen lying on the ground in the
dark whenever they cut back to the wide shot.

00:10:57.050 --> 00:11:01.290
Saw also featured some spiraling camera movements,
and I think we can count this as another example

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:03.000
of James Wan being an auteur.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:06.112
I noticed one such camera move back at the
house, not to mention the fact that they have

00:11:06.112 --> 00:11:07.440
a spiral staircase.

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:10.800
Detective Lipton does what probably anyone
would do in this scenario.

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:13.040
“Brutality!”

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:15.260
“Brutality!”

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:19.640
“Brutality! Brutality!”

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:21.520
“Brutality!”

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:28.760
“Brutality!”

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:32.000
But unfortunately for Lipton, he can’t stop
himself from screaming…

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:35.400
"AHHHHHH!!!!!"

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:38.680
...and he gets the old tongue-ectomy treatment.

00:11:38.680 --> 00:11:42.720
Jamie on the other hand is able to catch himself
before screaming, in mid-air might I add,

00:11:42.721 --> 00:11:46.960
and he burns the place down and goes home
one final time to take care of the last doll

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:50.400
and when he gets there, there’s one more
director’s trademark: the recap montage!

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:51.460
Let’s roll it.

00:11:51.860 --> 00:11:55.040
"Jamie, I can prove you didn't kill her."

00:11:57.700 --> 00:11:58.780
"Jamie!"

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:00.980
"Glad to have you home."

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:11.600
"Now who's the dummy?"

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:17.300
"NOOOOOOOO!!!!!!!!"

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:20.440
"Shhhhhhhhhh......."

00:12:20.460 --> 00:12:21.320
There’s a

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:24.520
good amount of lore in this movie that I’ve
yet to cover, so be sure to join me next week

00:12:24.520 --> 00:12:26.570
as I analyse the history of Mary Shaw.

00:12:26.570 --> 00:12:30.960
Remember to subscribe to CZsWorld for new
horrors every week, ring that deathbell for

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:33.780
notifications and I’ll see you in the next
one.

00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:36.200
Assuming we both survive.

